Virtual DOM

React keeps a copy of UI in memory.

This copy is called:

Virtual DOM

Example tree:

App
├── Navbar
├── Home
└── Footer

When state changes:

React creates NEW virtual tree
compares with OLD tree
finds differences
updates real DOM minimally

This comparison is reconciliation.

What is "Diffing"?

Diffing means:

finding differences

Example:

OLD:

<li>A</li>
<li>B</li>

NEW:

<li>A</li>
<li>C</li>

React detects:

B changed to C

Only updates that item.

What is Update?

Update means:

change in data/state/props

Usually from:

setState()

or

setCount()

Update → Re-render → Reconciliation → DOM update

Important Line

"React thinks as if whole app re-renders"

This means:

React behaves like:

"Let's rebuild everything again"

BUT internally React is smart.

It actually updates only changed parts.

So developers can write simple code without worrying about performance.